# New single characters needed (not yet GLOSS keys), with the sense used here:
#   俎 zǔ (a stand for meat offerings; 俎豆 Zhu Xi 禮器), 糧 liáng (grain, provisions), 窮 qióng (to be at the end of
#   one's means, in straits), 濫 làn (to overflow, run past bounds; 何氏 in Zhu Xi 濫溢也), 蠻 mán (the Man, peoples
#   of the south; Zhu Xi 南蠻), 貊 mò (the Mo, peoples of the north; Zhu Xi 北狄; 亡百反), 州 zhōu (a district;
#   Zhu Xi 二千五百家為州; Zheng Xuan 万二千五百家), 輿 yú (a carriage), 倚 yǐ (to lean on), 衡 héng (the yoke-bar
#   of a carriage; Zhu Xi 衡軛也), 巻 juǎn (the edition's form of 卷: to roll up, gather in; Zhu Xi 巻收也), 工 gōng
#   (a craftsman), 輅 lù (the great state carriage; Zhu Xi 輅音路…大車之名), 鄭 zhèng (Zheng, a state), 聲 shēng
#   (sound; music, 鄭聲 'the sounds of Zheng'), 栁 liǔ (the edition's form of 柳: willow; in 栁下惠), 責 zé (to blame,
#   to demand of), 羣 qún (the edition's form of 群: a crowd, in company), 慧 huì (cleverness; 小慧 Zhu Xi 私智).
# Existing entries that need broadening (for the glosser): 陳 (read zhèn 'battle array' = 陣, Zhu Xi 陳去聲，陳謂
#   軍師行伍之列, 15.1); 參 (cān 'to stand alongside as a third', Zhu Xi 七南反，與我相參也, 15.5); 矢 (already
#   'arrow'; 如矢 'straight as an arrow', 15.6); 懷 ('to store away', Zhu Xi 懷藏也, 15.6); 利 (verb 'to sharpen',
#   15.9); 時 ('the seasons, the calendar', 夏之時 15.10); 放 ('to banish, forbid', Zhu Xi 禁絶之, 15.10); 舞 (the
#   dance of a music, 韶舞 15.10); 厚 / 薄 ('heavy / light' of blame, 15.14); 質 ('trunk, substance', Zhu Xi 質幹,
#   15.17); 孫 (already xùn = 遜); 殆 ('dangerous', Zhu Xi 危也, 15.10); 興 (already 'to rise').
# Not bound, meaning what their parts mean: 俎 | 豆 (unsure — 8.4 binds 籩豆; listed for the run), 軍 | 旅 (as 14.20),
#   明 | 日, 志 | 士 and 仁 | 人 (Zhu Xi glosses each as a phrase: 有志之士, 成徳之人; listed for the run), 南 | 面
#   (as 6.1), 蠻 | 貊, 州 | 里, 鄭 | 聲, 佞 | 人, 逺 | 慮, 近 | 憂, 竊 | 位, 終 | 日 (as 2.9), 小 | 慧, 一 | 以 | 貫.
# Collation (Ruan 1816 KR1h0004; He Yan SBCK KR1h0005), noted where sense-bearing — Ruan: 恭己 (7), 不與言
#   without 之 (14). He Yan: 君子亦窮乎 without 有 (2); 汝 for 女 (3, same word); 參然於前 and 夫然後行也 (10);
#   可卷而懷也 (13); 不與言 / 而與言之 (14); 仁者也 (16); 人而無逺慮 (23); 遜 for 孫 (29, same word). Not noted
#   (graphic): 爼, 甞, 粮, 從, 愠, 巳, 鲜, 䔍, 㹮, 卷, 滛, 柳, 群.

UNITS_A = [
# ---------------- 15.1 ----------------
dict(pas="15.1", w="衛靈公=wèi líng gōng/Duke Ling of Wei//Wei (a state)//Ling (his posthumous name)//duke | 問/asked about | 陳=zhèn/battle array (= 陣) | 於/of | 孔子。=kǒng zǐ/Kongzi | ^孔子=kǒng zǐ/Kongzi | 對/replied | 曰：「/saying | ^俎=zǔ/(of) the meat-stands | 豆/(and) the offering dishes | 之/(genitive) | 事，/the matters | ^則/then | 嘗=cháng/once | 聞/(I) have heard | 之/(of) them | 矣；/(so it is) | ^軍/(of) the army | 旅/(and) the troops | 之/(genitive) | 事，/the matters | ^未/not yet | 之/them | 學/(have I) learned | 也。」/(assertion) | ^明/(the) next | 日/day | 遂/thereupon | 行。=xíng/(he) set off",
  l="Duke Ling of Wei asked Kongzi about battle array.\nKongzi replied: “The matters of stands and dishes —\nof those I have at some time heard;\nthe matters of armies and troops —\nthose I have not yet learned.”\nThe next day he set off.",
  i="Duke Ling of Wei asked Kongzi how troops should be drawn up for battle. Kongzi replied: “Of sacrificial vessels and how they are set out I have heard something; of armies and campaigns I have made no study.” The next day he left.",
  n="The book is named 衛靈公 from its opening words; Zhu Xi's heading counts forty-one chapters (凡四十一章), and our numbers are his. (Ruan Yuan's 1816 text starts a new chapter at 在陳絶糧 in the next unit, so from there its numbers run one ahead of ours.) 衛靈公 Duke Ling of Wei is the misruling lord of 14.20. 陳 here is read zhèn, the word later written 陣 'battle array' (Zhu Xi: 陳去聲; 陳謂軍師行伍之列 'the ranks and files of an army'); He Yan's Kong Anguo: the method of drawing up an army in ranks. In the next unit the same graph is chén, the state of Chen. 對 is the formal 'replied', used to a superior. 俎 zǔ is a stand for meat offerings, 豆 a stemmed dish; together, Zhu Xi says, vessels of the rites (禮器), and so the rites themselves. Each half of the answer is a topic, 'the matters of…', then a comment: 則 'then, as for that'; 嘗 cháng 'once, at some time'. 未之學 'not yet it learned': after a negative such as 未, a pronoun object (之) comes before the verb. He Yan's Zheng Xuan: 12,500 men make a 軍 and 500 a 旅; armies are a branch matter, and where the root is not yet established one cannot be taught the branches. Zhu Xi quotes Yin Tun (尹氏): Duke Ling was a lord without the Way, and on top of that had his mind on war and conquest, so the Master answered that he had not learned it, and left him. 遂 'thereupon'; 行 xíng 'to set out' — he left Wei."),
dict(w="在/(while) in | 陳，=chén/Chen | 絶/(they were) cut off from | 糧，=liáng/provisions | ^従=cóng/following (him) | 者/those | 病，/(were) worn out with illness | ^莫/none | 能/could | 興。=xīng/get up",
  l="In Chen they were cut off from provisions;\nthose following him fell ill,\nand none could get up.",
  i="In Chen their food ran out; his followers were so weak with hunger and sickness that none of them could stand.",
  n="陳 chén is the state of Chen, south of Lu — the same graph as the zhèn 'battle array' just above. Zhu Xi: Kongzi left Wei and went to Chen. 絶 is the edition's form of 絕 jué 'to be cut off from, to be without'; 糧 liáng is grain, provisions for a journey. 従者 'those following', the disciples travelling with him: Zhu Xi marks 従 去聲 (zòng), the reading for an attendant; modern practice reads cóng, and so do we (as 11.2). 病 'ill, exhausted'. 莫能 'none could'; 興 xīng 'to rise' (Zhu Xi: 興起也). He Yan's Kong Anguo: 從者 are the disciples, and 興 is to rise; Kongzi left Wei for Cao, Cao would not receive him; he went on to Song and met the trouble with the men of Kuang (遭匡人之難); then he went to Chen, and when Wu attacked Chen, Chen was in disorder, and so they ran short of food. Ruan Yuan's 1816 text begins its 15.2 here."),
dict(w="子路=zǐ lù/Zilu | 慍=yùn/resentful | 見=xiàn/came before (him) | 曰：「/(and) said | ^君子=jūn zǐ/the gentleman | 亦/too | 有/does (he) have | 窮=qióng/being at the end of his means, straits | 乎？」/(question) | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^君子=jūn zǐ/the gentleman | 固/surely, indeed | 窮，=qióng/(has his times) in straits | ^小人=xiǎo rén/the small man | 窮=qióng/in straits | 斯/then | 濫=làn/spills over (all bounds) | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="Zilu came before him, resentful, and said: “Does the gentleman too have his straits?”\nThe Master said: “The gentleman indeed has his straits;\nthe small man in straits spills over.”",
  i="Zilu came to the Master smarting and said: “Does even a gentleman come to the end of his road?” The Master said: “Of course a gentleman comes to the end of his road at times. It is the small man who, once there, throws off all restraint.”",
  n="慍 yùn 'resentful, aggrieved', as in 1.1. 見 is xiàn 'to come before, present oneself' (Zhu Xi: 賢遍反). 亦 'too' — the gentleman as well? 窮 qióng is to come to the end, to have no way through: the opposite of getting through (達). In the answer 固 can be taken two ways, and Zhu Xi gives both. He quotes a Mr He (何氏): 濫 is to overflow; the gentleman surely has his times of straits, unlike the small man, who in straits overflows and does wrong (放溢為非) — words that are, nearly verbatim, the unnamed comment in He Yan's collection. Then Master Cheng: 固窮 is 'holds firm in his straits' (固守其窮), which, Zhu Xi says, also works (亦通). 斯 'then'. Zhu Xi's own word: the sage goes when he should go, without misgivings, and in hardship comes through (處困而亨) without resentment or regret; this can be seen here, and learners should savour it deeply. Variant: He Yan's text reads 君子亦窮乎, without 有."),
# ---------------- 15.2 ----------------
dict(pas="15.2", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^賜/Ci | 也，/(calling pause) | ^女=rǔ/you | 以/take | 予=yú/me | 為=wéi/to be | 多/much | 學/learning | 而/and | 識=zhì/keeping in memory | 之/it | 者/one who | 與？」=yú/(question)",
  l="The Master said: “Ci,\ndo you take me for one who learns much and keeps it in memory?”",
  i="The Master said: “Ci, do you think of me as a man who has learned a great many things and remembers them all?”",
  n="賜 Ci is Zigong's personal name (1.15), used by his teacher; 也 after a name in address is a calling pause. 女 is rǔ 'you' (Zhu Xi: 女音汝; He Yan's text writes 汝). 以X為Y 'take X to be Y'; 予 yú 'I, me'. 識 is zhì 'to remember, keep in mind' (Zhu Xi: 識音志), as in 7.2. The whole of 多學而識之 is closed by 者 'one who…'. 與 is yú, a question particle (Zhu Xi: 與平聲, and 'the same below', 下同). Zhu Xi: Zigong's learning was wide and he could remember it; the Master wanted him to know what it all rests on, so he asked, to draw him out."),
dict(w="對/(Zigong) replied | 曰：「/saying | ^然，/so it is | ^非/is it not (so) | 與？」=yú/(question)",
  l="He replied: “Yes.\nIs it not so?”",
  i="“Yes,” he answered. “Is that not so?”",
  n="然 'so it is, yes'. 非與 'is it not?' — 與 yú again the question particle. Zhu Xi: just as he was agreeing, he suddenly doubted — presumably because his long work at learning had brought him far, and he too was about to get something. He Yan's Kong Anguo: 然 means he did think him one who learns much and remembers; 非與 asks, 'Is it now not so?' (問今不然邪也)."),
dict(w="曰：「/(the Master) said | ^非/(it is) not (so) | 也，/(assertion) | ^予=yú/I | 一/one (thing) | 以/thereby | 貫=guàn/string through | 之。」/it",
  l="He said: “No.\nWith me, one thing strings it through.”",
  i="The Master said: “No. There is one thing that runs through all of it.”",
  n="非也 'it is not (so)'. 予一以貫之 'I, by one (thing), string it through': 一以 is 以一 'by means of one' with the object put first, as in 4.15 吾道一以貫之, whose English we keep. 貫 'to string together, run through' (Zhu Xi at 4.15: 通也). Zhu Xi: the saying is explained at Book 4; but there it is spoken of conduct, here of knowledge (彼以行言而此以知言也). He quotes Xie Liangzuo: the sage's Way is great and men cannot take it all in, so naturally they think it much learning and memory; but how should the sage be one who works at breadth? He is like Heaven with its many forms — it does not carve them thing by thing. Then two lines of the Odes: virtue is light as a hair, yet a hair still has its like; the doings of high Heaven have neither sound nor smell (德輶如毛，毛猶有倫；上天之載，無聲無臭) — 'that is the utmost'. And Yin Tun: to Master Zeng the Master said this straight out, without waiting to be asked, and Master Zeng understood it deeply and answered 'Yes' (4.15); with Zigong he first drew out the doubt and then told him, and Zigong never could answer 'Yes' as Master Zeng did — here the depth of the two men's learning can be seen. Zhu Xi adds that the Master often drew Zigong out in this way, and others had no part in it, so the depth of learning of the disciples below Yan Hui and Master Zeng can be seen as well. He Yan's comment (unnamed): good has its source and affairs their meeting point; the world has different roads but one destination, a hundred thoughts but one end (天下殊塗而同歸，百慮而一致); know the source and all the good is in hand — so there is no need of much learning: by one thing he knows it."),
# ---------------- 15.3 ----------------
dict(pas="15.3", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^由！=yóu/Yóu | ^知=zhī/knowing | 德=dé/virtue | 者/those who | 鮮=xiǎn/(are) few | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master said: “Yóu!\nThose who know virtue are few.”",
  i="The Master said: “Yóu, there are not many who understand virtue.”",
  n="由 Yóu is Zilu's personal name (Zhu Xi: the Master calls Zilu by his name to tell him this), as in 2.17. 知德者 'those who know virtue': 者 closes the phrase. The edition prints 德 here and its usual 徳 elsewhere; the word is the same. 鮮 is xiǎn 'few' (Zhu Xi: 鮮上聲), and 矣 marks the state of things. Zhu Xi: 德 is what one has got of right and principle in oneself (義理之得於己者); unless one has it oneself, one cannot know its real savour. He adds that everything from the first chapter of the book to here was perhaps said at one time (自弟一章至此疑皆一時之言 — the print has 弟 for 第), and that this chapter was probably spoken because of Zilu's resentful appearance in 15.1. He Yan's Wang Su says the same: the gentleman is firm in straits, yet Zilu came before him resentful; so the Master told him there are few who know virtue."),
# ---------------- 15.4 ----------------
dict(pas="15.4", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^無/without | 為=wéi/doing (anything) | 而/and yet | 治=zhì/governing well | 者，/the one who | ^其/surely it was | 舜/Shun | 也/(assertion) | 與！=yú/(exclamation) | ^夫=fú/now | 何/what | 為=wéi/did (he) do | 哉？/(question) | ^恭/(he) made reverent | 己/himself | 正/(and) squarely | 南=nán/south | 面/faced | 而/and | 已=yǐ/that was all | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master said: “One who governed well without doing anything —\nthat surely was Shun!\nNow what did he do?\nHe made himself reverent and faced squarely south, and that was all.”",
  i="The Master said: “If ever a man kept the realm in order while doing nothing, it was Shun. And what did he do? He held himself in reverence and took his seat looking south — nothing more.”",
  n="The print has 恭已, a misprint for 恭己, corrected here. The Siku copy confuses 己 'oneself', 已 'to stop' and 巳 throughout (as at 4.14 and 5.15); Zhu Xi's own comment on the line has 恭己者聖人敬德之容, Ruan Yuan's 1816 text reads 恭己, and He Yan's text prints 巳, its form for both. 'Made himself reverent' is the only sense the line allows; the final 而已矣 'and that is all' is 已 yǐ, as printed. 無為而治者 'the one who, without doing, governed well' (為 wéi, 治 zhì): 者 closes the whole phrase. 其…也與 'surely it was…!' — 與 yú (Zhu Xi: 平聲). 夫 fú opens the question, 'now' (Zhu Xi: 音扶). 恭己 is verb and object, 'made himself reverent'; 南面 'to face south', the ruler's seat (6.1). Zhu Xi: governing without doing is the sage's virtue so abundant that the people are transformed without his needing to do anything. Shun alone is named because he came after Yao and also got the right men to fill all the offices, so still less trace of his doing is seen. 恭己 is the bearing of the sage reverent in virtue; since he did nothing, this was all that people saw. He Yan's comment (unnamed): he gave the offices to the right men, and so governed without doing."),
# ---------------- 15.5 ----------------
dict(pas="15.5", w="子張=zǐ zhāng/Zizhang | 問/asked about | 行。=xíng/getting through",
  l="Zizhang asked about getting through.",
  i="Zizhang asked how a man could make his way wherever he went.",
  n="子張 Zizhang is Zhuansun Shi 顓孫師 (2.18). 行 xíng here is 'to go, to get through' — to have one's way and meet no block. Zhu Xi: it is like asking about 達 'making one's way' (猶問達之意也), the question Zizhang asks in 12.20."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^言/(in) speech | 忠/wholehearted | 信，/(and) true to one's word | ^行=xíng/(in) conduct | 篤/solid | 敬，/(and) reverent | ^雖/even | 蠻=mán/(among) the Man | 貊=mò/(and) the Mo | 之/(genitive) | 邦，/states | 行=xíng/(one) gets through | 矣；/(so it is) | ^言/(in) speech | 不/not | 忠/wholehearted | 信，/(and) true to one's word | ^行=xíng/(in) conduct | 不/not | 篤/solid | 敬，/(and) reverent | ^雖/even | 州=zhōu/(in one's own) district | 里，/(and) village | 行=xíng/(does one) get through | 乎/(question) | 哉？」/(exclamation)",
  l="The Master said: “In speech wholehearted and true to one's word,\nin conduct solid and reverent —\neven in the states of the Man and the Mo, one gets through;\nin speech not wholehearted and true to one's word,\nin conduct not solid and reverent —\neven in one's own district and village, does one get through?”",
  i="The Master said: “Let your words be wholehearted and true and your conduct steady and respectful, and you will find your way even among the tribes of the south and the north. If your words are not wholehearted and true, and your conduct not steady and respectful, will you find your way even in your own neighbourhood?”",
  n="Two halves of one shape: two conditions, then 雖 'even' + a place + 行 'gets through'. Zhu Xi marks the 行 of 行篤 and 行不 去聲, xìng, the noun 'conduct'; modern practice reads xíng, and so do we (as 14.4). The other two 行 are the verb of Zizhang's question, 'to get through'. 忠 'wholehearted' and 信 'good faith' are the pair of 1.4; 篤 is 'solid, thick' (Zhu Xi: 篤厚也), 敬 'reverent'. Zhu Xi: 蠻 mán are the Man of the south and 貊 mò (亡百反) the Di of the north (蠻南蠻貊北狄) — peoples outside the Chinese states. 州 zhōu and 里 are units of one's own home ground: Zhu Xi, 2,500 households make a 州; He Yan's Zheng Xuan, 12,500 households make a 州, and five households a 鄰, five 鄰 a 里. 行乎哉 'does one get through?' expects no — Zheng Xuan: it means one cannot. Zhu Xi: Zizhang's thought was of getting his way out in the world, so the Master turned him back to himself, as in his answers about seeking a salary (2.18) and making one's way (12.20)."),
dict(w="立/standing | 則/then | 見=jiàn/(one) sees | 其/them | 參=cān/standing alongside | 於/in | 前/front | 也；/(assertion) | ^在/(being) in | 輿=yú/the carriage | 則/then | 見=jiàn/(one) sees | 其/them | 倚=yǐ/leaning | 於/on | 衡=héng/the yoke-bar | 也；/(assertion) | ^夫=fú/now | 然/(it being) so | 後/after that | 行。=xíng/(one) gets through",
  l="Standing, one sees them ranged before one;\nin the carriage, one sees them leaning on the yoke-bar —\nthen, and only then, one gets through.",
  i="When you stand, see them there in front of you; when you ride in your carriage, see them resting on the yoke before you. Only then will you get through.",
  n="The Master's answer runs on past Zhu Xi's comment and is left unquoted. 其 'them' — Zhu Xi: it points to the four things just named, wholeheartedness, good faith, solidity and reverence. 參 is read cān (Zhu Xi: 七南反); he says it is the 參 of the saying 'do not go and make a third' (母往參焉; the print has 母 for 毋), meaning they stand alongside one (與我相參也). 輿 yú is a carriage, and 衡 héng the crossbar of the yoke at the front (Zhu Xi: 衡軛也). 見 here is jiàn 'to see'. 夫 fú (Zhu Xi: 音扶) opens the last clause: 'now, it being so, after that — one gets through'. Zhu Xi: one never forgets them in any thought; wherever one is, it is always as if one saw them; though one wished to leave them for a moment one could not; then every word and act will of itself not leave them, and one can get through even among the Man and the Mo. He Yan's Bao Xian: 衡 is the yoke; thinking always of wholeheartedness and good faith, standing, one seems to see them 'ranged' (參然) before one, and in the carriage, as if leaning on the yoke. Variant: He Yan's text reads 立則見其參然於前也 'ranged before one' and ends 夫然後行也, adding 也."),
dict(w="子張=zǐ zhāng/Zizhang | 書/wrote | 諸=zhū/it on (= 之於) | 紳。=shēn/(his) sash",
  l="Zizhang wrote it on his sash.",
  i="Zizhang wrote the saying on the end of his sash.",
  n="諸 is the fused 之於 'it on'. 紳 shēn is the great sash of formal dress (10.13); Zhu Xi: the hanging end of the great sash (紳大帶之垂者), and he wrote it there because he wished not to forget it. He Yan's Kong Anguo: 紳 is the great sash. Zhu Xi quotes Master Cheng: learning must press close in upon oneself; 'learn broadly and be firm in aim, ask closely and think of what is near' (19.6), and the Master's words here — 'in speech wholehearted and true, in conduct solid and reverent; standing, see them ranged before you; in the carriage, see them leaning on the yoke' — this is learning itself. One of fine stuff sees through it fully, the dregs melt away, and he becomes one body with Heaven and Earth; the next best keep and nourish it by gravity and reverence; when they arrive, it is one and the same."),
# ---------------- 15.6 ----------------
dict(pas="15.6", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^直/straight | 哉/indeed | 史魚！=shǐ yú/Shi Yu — the archivist Yu, a grandee of Wei//archivist, scribe (his office)//fish (Yu, his name) | ^邦/(when) the state | 有/had | 道，=dào/the Way | 如/(he was) like | 矢；/an arrow | ^邦/(when) the state | 無/lacked | 道，=dào/the Way | 如/(he was) like | 矢。」/an arrow",
  l="The Master said: “Straight indeed, Shi Yu!\nWhen the state had the Way, like an arrow;\nwhen the state lacked the Way, like an arrow.”",
  i="The Master said: “How straight Shi Yu was! When the state was well governed he went straight as an arrow, and when it was badly governed he went straight as an arrow still.”",
  n="直哉史魚 'straight — how! — Shi Yu': in an exclamation the predicate comes first, as in 9.2 大哉孔子. Zhu Xi: 史 is the name of his office, the archivist; 魚 is a grandee of Wei, whose personal name was Qiu 鰌 (史官名，魚衛大夫名鰌); He Yan's Kong Anguo: the Wei grandee Shi Qiu (史鰌). 如矢 'like an arrow' — Zhu Xi: it means straight. He adds the story behind the praise: Shi Yu, because he had failed to bring forward the worthy and send away the unworthy, still remonstrated with his lord after death, by means of his corpse (尸諫); the affair is in the Kongzi jiayu (家語). Kong Anguo: whether the state had the Way or not, his conduct was straight as an arrow and did not bend."),
dict(w="君子=jūn zǐ/a gentleman | 哉/indeed | 蘧伯玉！=qú bó yù/Qu Boyu | ^邦/(when) the state | 有/had | 道，=dào/the Way | 則/then | 仕；/(he) took office | ^邦/(when) the state | 無/lacked | 道，=dào/the Way | 則/then | 可/(he) could | 巻=juǎn/roll (it) up | 而/and | 懷=huái/put away in his breast | 之。/it",
  l="A gentleman indeed, Qu Boyu!\nWhen the state had the Way, he took office;\nwhen the state lacked the Way, he could roll it up and keep it in his breast.",
  i="And what a gentleman Qu Boyu was! When the state was well governed he took office; when it was badly governed he could fold away what he had to offer and keep it to himself.",
  n="The Master's words run on past Zhu Xi's comment and are left unquoted. 君子哉 + name is the shape of 5.2 'A gentleman indeed, this man!'. 蘧伯玉 Qu Boyu is the grandee of Wei of 14.26. 仕 'to take office'. 巻 juǎn is the edition's form of 卷 'to roll up'; Zhu Xi: 巻收也 'to gather in', 懷藏也 'to store away'. 之 'it' is not named: whatever he had to give. 可 'could' — it was in his power to do so. Zhu Xi: Boyu's going out to serve and staying home both fitted the sage's Way, so he is called a gentleman; such was his giving no answer and leaving when Sun Linfu and Ning Zhi plotted the expulsion and murder of their rulers (如於孫林父甯殖放弑之謀不對而出亦其事也). He quotes Yang Shi: Shi Yu's straightness did not reach the whole of the gentleman's Way; only one like Qu Boyu could come through a disordered age unharmed — with Shi Yu's arrow-straightness, even had he wished to roll it up and put it away, he could not have. He Yan's Bao Xian: to roll up and put away means taking no part in the government of the day — yielding, and giving offence to no one. Variant: He Yan's text reads 可卷而懷也, with 也 for 之."),
# ---------------- 15.7 ----------------
dict(pas="15.7", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^可/(one who) can (be) | 與=yǔ/with | 言/talked | 而/and yet | 不/not | 與=yǔ/with | 之/him | 言，/to talk | ^失/(is to) lose | 人；/the man | ^不/(one who) cannot | 可/(be) | 與=yǔ/with | 言/talked | 而/and yet | 與=yǔ/with | 之/him | 言，/to talk | ^失/(is to) lose | 言。/(one's) words | ^知=zhì/the wise | 者/one | 不/does not | 失/lose | 人，/men | ^亦/also | 不/does not | 失/lose | 言。」/words",
  l="The Master said: “One who could be talked with, and you do not talk with him —\nyou lose the man;\none who could not be talked with, and you talk with him —\nyou lose your words.\nThe wise one loses no man,\nand loses no words either.”",
  i="The Master said: “If a man would repay your talking with him and you hold your tongue, you have lost him; if he would not and you talk to him anyway, your words are thrown away. One who is wise loses neither the man nor his words.”",
  n="可與言 'can be talked with': 與 yǔ 'with', its object (the man) left unsaid; then 與之言 'talk with him', 之 'him'. Each half is a condition and a short verdict, 失人 'lose the man', 失言 'lose the words'. 知者 'the wise one', 知 zhì (Zhu Xi: 知去聲). Zhu Xi gives only the sound note here, and He Yan has no comment. Variants: Ruan Yuan's 1816 text reads 可與言而不與言, without 之; He Yan's text has the same, and in the second half 而與言之 'and talk with him', with 之 after the verb."),
# ---------------- 15.8 ----------------
dict(pas="15.8", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^志/purposeful | 士，/officers | 仁/humane | 人，/men | ^無/(there are) none (who) | 求/seek | 生/life | 以/so as to | 害/harm | 仁；/humaneness | ^有/there are (those who) | 殺=shā/kill | 身/(their own) person | 以/so as to | 成/complete | 仁。」/humaneness",
  l="The Master said: “The officer of purpose, the humane man:\nnone seeks life at the cost of harming humaneness;\nsome give up their lives to make humaneness complete.”",
  i="The Master said: “No man of firm purpose, no humane man, will buy his life by doing harm to humaneness; there have been those who went to their deaths so that humaneness might be made whole.”",
  n="志士 'an officer of purpose' — Zhu Xi: an officer who has a will set (有志之士); 仁人 'a humane man' — Zhu Xi: a man of completed virtue (成徳之人). The two are the topic; then 無… 'there are none who…' and 有… 'there are those who…'. 以 'so as to, and thereby'. 殺身 'to kill one's own person': 殺 shā, 身 'oneself'. Zhu Xi: when by principle one ought to die and yet seeks life, there is unease in one's heart — that harms the virtue of the heart; when one ought to die and dies, the heart is at ease and virtue is whole. He quotes Master Cheng: real principle, once got in the heart, is of itself different; real principle is truly seeing right as right and wrong as wrong; those of old who gave up their bodies and lost their lives could not have done it without truly seeing, and they must have seen that life is not weightier than what is right, and that living is not more at ease than dying; so 'there are those who kill themselves to complete humaneness' — it is only making one 'right' complete (成就一箇是而已). He Yan's Kong Anguo: they do not seek life and harm humaneness; they die, and so complete humaneness — the officer of purpose and the humane man do not cling to their own lives."),
# ---------------- 15.9 ----------------
dict(pas="15.9", w="子貢=zǐ gòng/Zigong | 問/asked about | 為=wéi/practising | 仁。/humaneness | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^工=gōng/a craftsman | 欲/wishing | 善/to do well | 其/his | 事，/work | ^必/must | 先/first | 利/put an edge on | 其/his | 器。/tools | ^居/living | 是/(in) this | 邦/state | 也，/(pause) | ^事/serve | 其/its | 大夫=dà fū/grandees | 之/(genitive) | 賢/worthy | 者，/ones | ^友/befriend | 其/its | 士/officers | 之/(genitive) | 仁/humane | 者。」/ones",
  l="Zigong asked about practising humaneness.\nThe Master said: “A craftsman who would make a good job of his work\nmust first put an edge on his tools.\nLiving in a given state,\nserve the worthy among its grandees,\nbefriend the humane among its officers.”",
  i="Zigong asked how to go about being humane. The Master said: “A workman who means his work to turn out well sees first to the edge of his tools. In whatever state you live, take service with those of its grandees who are worthy, and make friends of those of its officers who are humane.”",
  n="為仁 wéi rén 'practising humaneness' — Zhu Xi quotes Master Cheng: Zigong asked about practising humaneness, not about humaneness itself, so Kongzi told him only what one practises it with (為仁之資). 工 gōng is a craftsman. 善其事 'make his work good'; 利 'sharp' used as a verb, 'to sharpen'. 是邦 'this state' — whatever state one lives in. 大夫之賢者 'the worthy ones among the grandees', 士之仁者 'the humane ones among the officers': X之Y者 picks the Y ones out of X. Zhu Xi: 'worthy' is said with regard to affairs, 'humane' with regard to virtue; the Master once said that Zigong liked those not up to himself (悦不若己者), so he told him this, wishing him to have men to stand in awe of and to be cut and polished by (嚴憚切磋), and so to complete his virtue. He Yan's Kong Anguo: as the craftsman works with sharp tools, a man takes worthy friends as his help. Variant: He Yan's text ends 友其士之仁者也, adding 也."),
# ---------------- 15.10 ----------------
dict(pas="15.10", w="顔淵=yán yuān/Yan Yuan | 問/asked about | 為=wéi/governing | 邦。/a state",
  l="Yan Yuan asked about governing a state.",
  i="Yan Yuan asked how a state should be governed.",
  n="顔淵 Yan Yuan is Yan Hui, the disciple the Master rated highest. 為邦 'governing a state', 為 wéi, as 13.11. Zhu Xi: Yanzi had the gifts of a king's chief helper (王佐之才), so he asked about the Way of ordering the world; saying 'a state' is the modest form of words (謙辭)."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^行=xíng/use | 夏/the Xia | 之/(genitive) | 時。」/seasons",
  l="The Master said: “Use the seasons of the Xia.”",
  i="The Master said: “Keep the calendar of the Xia.”",
  n="The answer comes in four short orders, each with Zhu Xi's comment after it; the next four units carry on the Master's words and are left unquoted. 行 xíng 'to put into use'. 夏 Xia is the first of the Three Dynasties; 時 'the seasons', the calendar. Zhu Xi: the Xia seasons take as the head of the year the month in which, at first dusk, the handle of the Dipper points to yin 寅. Heaven opened at zi 子, earth at chou 丑, man was born at yin 寅, so the months in which the handle points to any of these three could head the year, and the Three Dynasties used them in turn: the Xia took yin, man's standard; the Shang chou, earth's; the Zhou zi, Heaven's. But the seasons are for doing work, so the year should be reckoned by man. That is why Kongzi once said, 'I got the seasons of Xia' (吾得夏時焉), which expounders take to mean such works as the Xia xiaozheng (夏小正); he chose it for the rightness of its seasons and the goodness of its ordinances, and here told Yanzi the same. He Yan's comment (unnamed): it was based on seeing the birth of the ten thousand things, which it made the beginning of the four seasons, and he chose it because it is easy to know."),
dict(w="乘=chéng/ride | 殷/the Yin | 之/(genitive) | 輅。=lù/state carriage",
  l="Ride the state carriage of the Yin.",
  i="Ride in the carriage of the Yin.",
  n="乘 is chéng 'to ride' here, not shèng 'a chariot'. 殷 Yin is the Shang dynasty. 輅 lù is a great carriage (Zhu Xi: 輅音路, also written 路). Zhu Xi: the Shang carriage was a wooden one; 輅 is the name of the great carriage; in antiquity carriages were simply made of wood, and the name 輅 appears with the Shang, which perhaps first gave it a distinct form. The Zhou decorated theirs with gold and jade, which was too lavish and easily spoiled — not as good as the Shang carriage, plain, whole and solid, while rank was already marked: substance, and the mean struck. He Yan's Ma Rong: the Yin carriage was called the Great Lu (大輅), and the Zuo zhuan's 'the Great Lu, with rush matting' (大輅越席) shows its frugality."),
dict(w="服=fú/wear | 周/the Zhou | 之/(genitive) | 冕。=miǎn/ceremonial cap",
  l="Wear the ceremonial cap of the Zhou.",
  i="Wear the ceremonial cap of the Zhou.",
  n="服 'clothes', used as a verb, 'to wear'. 冕 is the ceremonial cap, as in 9.3. Zhu Xi: the Zhou had five kinds of 冕, the caps of sacrificial dress, with a board on top and strings of pendants front and back; something of the kind had existed since the Yellow Emperor, but its rules and grades were first complete under the Zhou. Yet it is a small thing set above the whole body, so though ornate it is not wasteful and though costly not extravagant; the Master chose it, perhaps, as pattern that strikes the mean. He Yan's Bao Xian: the 冕 is the cap of the rites; the Zhou rites were patterned and complete; and it was chosen for its yellow silk-floss ear-stoppers (黈纊塞耳), not giving free rein to eyes and ears (不任視聴)."),
dict(w="樂=yuè/(for) music | 則/then | 韶=sháo/the Shao | 舞。/(and) its dances",
  l="For music, the Shao and its dances.",
  i="For music, the Shao of Shun, with its dances.",
  n="樂 is yuè 'music'. 則 after a topic: 'as for music, then…'. 韶 the Shao is the music of Shun (3.25, 7.13); 舞 'dance' — music and dance went together in performance. Zhu Xi: he chose it for being wholly good and wholly beautiful (取其盡善盡美), the words of 3.25. He Yan's comment (unnamed): the Shao is Shun's music, wholly good and wholly beautiful, so he chose it."),
dict(w="放/banish | 鄭=zhèng/(the) Zheng | 聲，=shēng/sounds | ^逺=yuǎn/keep far away | 佞=nìng/glib | 人。/men | ^鄭=zhèng/(the) Zheng | 聲=shēng/sounds | 淫，/(run) to excess | ^佞=nìng/glib | 人/men | 殆。/(are) dangerous",
  l="Banish the sounds of Zheng,\nkeep glib men far away:\nthe sounds of Zheng run to excess,\nglib men are a danger.",
  i="Ban the music of Zheng and keep smooth talkers at a distance. The music of Zheng leads to excess, and smooth talkers are a danger.",
  n="Two orders, then the reason for each. 放 is 'to banish' — Zhu Xi: to forbid and cut it off (禁絶之). 鄭聲 'the sounds of Zheng', Zhu Xi: the music of the state of Zheng. 逺 is the edition's form of 遠, here the verb 'to keep at a distance'; Zhu Xi marks it 去聲, yuàn, a reading modern practice does not keep (we print yuǎn). 佞人 Zhu Xi: men low, fawning and glib (卑諂辯給之人); 殆 'dangerous' (Zhu Xi: 殆危也). Zhu Xi quotes Master Cheng: many asked about government, but only to Yan Yuan did the Master answer like this; the institutions of the Three Dynasties each added and cut according to the times, and in time could not be free of faults; with the Zhou in decline and no sage arising, Kongzi weighed the rites of the former kings and set up a Way to be practised for ten thousand ages, giving this as its outline — from it the rest can be worked out. Zhang Zai: rites and music are the method of ordering; banishing the Zheng sounds and keeping glib men away is the intent beyond the method — one day's carelessness, and the method is broken; and again: when a method is set up and can be kept, virtue can last and the work grow great, and Zheng sounds and glib men can make men lose what they keep, so they must be banished and kept away. Yin Tun: this is the great method no king in a hundred could change; Kongzi's making of the Chunqiu was perhaps in this spirit; though Kongzi and Yan Hui could not put it into practice in their day, their method of order can still be seen. He Yan's Kong Anguo: Zheng sounds and glib men can move men's hearts just as elegant music and worthy men do, but they bring men to excess, disorder and danger; therefore they must be banished and kept away."),
# ---------------- 15.11 ----------------
dict(pas="15.11", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^人/a man | 無/without | 逺=yuǎn/far-reaching | 慮，/thought | ^必/surely | 有/has | 近/close-by | 憂。」/cares",
  l="The Master said: “A man with no thought that reaches far\nis sure to have cares close by.”",
  i="The Master said: “If a man's foresight does not reach far, trouble will soon be at his elbow.”",
  n="逺 yuǎn 'far' (the edition's form of 遠) is here an adjective, 'far-reaching', set against 近 'near'. 無X，必有Y 'without X, (he) surely has Y'. Zhu Xi quotes Su (蘇氏): of the ground a man treads on, all beyond the room for his feet is useless, yet it cannot be done away with; so if thought does not reach beyond a thousand li, trouble lies under the table and mat (几席之下). He Yan has no comment. Variant: He Yan's text reads 人而無逺慮 'a man, if without far thought', with 而."),
# ---------------- 15.12 ----------------
dict(pas="15.12", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^已=yǐ/finished | 矣/(so it is) | 乎！/(exclamation) | ^吾/I | 未/have not yet | 見=jiàn/seen | 好=hào/(one) delighting in | 徳=dé/virtue | 如/as | 好=hào/(he) delights in | 色/beauty | 者/the one who | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “It is finished!\nI have not yet seen one delighting in virtue as he delights in beauty.”",
  i="The Master said: “There is no hope of it. Never yet have I met a man who cared for virtue the way men care for a pretty face.”",
  n="The second line is 9.17 word for word, and its English is kept; the cry before it is the 已矣乎 'it is finished!' of 5.26. 已 yǐ 'to stop, be over'; 矣 and 乎 make it an exclamation. 好 is hào 'to be fond of' both times (Zhu Xi: 好去聲); 徳 is the edition's form of 德. Zhu Xi: 已矣乎 sighs that in the end he will never get to see it. He Yan has no comment here."),
# ---------------- 15.13 ----------------
dict(pas="15.13", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^臧文仲=zāng wén zhòng/Zang Wenzhong | 其/surely | 竊/stole | 位/(his) post | 者/one who | 與！=yú/(exclamation) | ^知=zhī/(he) knew | 栁下惠=liǔ xià huì/Liuxia Hui — Zhan Huo, a grandee of Lu//willow//below (Liuxia, his fief)//kind (Hui, his posthumous name) | 之/'s | 賢/worth | 而/and yet | 不/did not | 與=yǔ/with (him) | 立/stand | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “Zang Wenzhong — surely one who stole his post!\nHe knew Liuxia Hui's worth, and yet did not stand with him.”",
  i="The Master said: “Zang Wenzhong held his office like a thief. He knew what Liuxia Hui was worth, yet never brought him to stand beside him at court.”",
  n="臧文仲 Zang Wenzhong is the grandee of Lu of 5.17. 其…者與 'surely (he is) one who…!': 其 'surely', 與 yú (Zhu Xi: 者與之與平聲). 竊位 'to steal a post' — Zhu Xi: one not fit for his post and ashamed of it in his heart, like a thief who has got something and holds it in secret. 栁 is the edition's form of 柳 liǔ. Zhu Xi: Liuxia Hui was a grandee of Lu, Zhan Huo 展獲, courtesy name Qin 禽; his fief was Liuxia, and his posthumous name Hui (栁下惠魯大夫展獲字禽食邑栁下諡曰惠). 與立 'stand with', 與 yǔ — Zhu Xi: to stand side by side with him at court. He quotes Fan Zuyu: Zang Wenzhong governed Lu; not to know the worthy is a failure of sight, to know them and not raise them is to keep the worthy hidden; the first is the smaller fault, the second the greater; so Kongzi called him not humane, and also a stealer of his post. He Yan's Kong Anguo: Liuxia Hui is Zhan Qin; knowing he was worthy and not raising him is stealing a post."),
# ---------------- 15.14 ----------------
dict(pas="15.14", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^躬/(in) one's own person | 自/on oneself | 厚/heavy | 而/and | 薄/light | 責=zé/(in) blaming | 於/(toward) | 人，/others | ^則/then | 逺=yuǎn/(one) keeps far off | 怨/resentment | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master said: “Heavy on one's own self, and light in blaming others —\nthen one keeps resentment far off.”",
  i="The Master said: “Lay the heavy blame on yourself and only light blame on others, and no one will bear you a grudge.”",
  n="躬 'one's own person' and 自 'oneself' stress the self; 厚 'heavy, thick' and 薄 'thin, light' are the pair. 責 zé 'to blame, to demand of' is written once and serves both halves: Zhu Xi reads the first as 'blaming oneself heavily' (責己厚) and the second as 'blaming others lightly' (責人薄). 則 'then'. 逺 here is again the verb 'to keep at a distance', which Zhu Xi marks 去聲 (yuàn); we print the modern yuǎn, as at 15.10. Zhu Xi: blaming himself heavily, his own person is the better cultivated; blaming others lightly, others find it easy to follow him; and so they cannot resent him. He Yan's Kong Anguo: to blame oneself heavily and others lightly is the way to keep resentment and blame (怨咎) far away."),
# ---------------- 15.15 ----------------
dict(pas="15.15", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^不/(one who) does not | 曰『/say | 如之何，=rú zhī hé/what to do about it//like//it//what | 如之何』=rú zhī hé/what to do about it//like//it//what | 者，/the one who | ^吾/I | 末=mò/have no (way of) | 如之何=rú zhī hé/doing anything about him//like//him//what | 也/(assertion) | 已=yǐ/(and that is all) | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master said: “One who does not say ‘What to do about it? What to do about it?’ —\nwith him I have no way of doing anything, and that is all.”",
  i="The Master said: “If a man never asks himself ‘What should I do about this? What should I do?’, I am at a loss what to do about him.”",
  n="如之何 'what to do about it?' is the bound phrase of 9.23, used twice as the words a man might say and once as the Master's own; the closing 吾末如之何也已矣 is 9.23's, and its English is kept. 末 mò 'no, there is no way' (= 無). The whole of 不曰…者 'one who does not say…' is the topic. Zhu Xi: 'what to do about it, what to do about it' are the words of one who thinks a thing through and weighs how to handle it (熟思而審處之辭); one who does not, and acts recklessly — even a sage can do nothing with him. He Yan's Kong Anguo: not saying 如之何 is like not saying 'what can be done about this?' (奈是何); and on the last words: when calamity has already taken shape, I too can do nothing about it."),
# ---------------- 15.16 ----------------
dict(pas="15.16", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^羣=qún/in a crowd | 居/staying together | 終/the whole | 日，/day | ^言/(their) talk | 不/does not | 及/reach | 義，/what is right | ^好=hào/(they are) fond of | 行=xíng/practising | 小/petty | 慧，=huì/cleverness | ^難=nán/hard | 矣/(so it is) | 哉！」/(exclamation)",
  l="The Master said: “Together in a crowd the whole day,\ntheir talk never reaching what is right,\nfond of practising petty cleverness —\nhard indeed!”",
  i="The Master said: “A pack of people who spend all day together, whose talk never touches on what is right, and who love to show off their little tricks of wit — it is hard to make anything of them!”",
  n="羣 qún is the edition's form of 群 'a herd, a crowd'; 羣居 'to stay together in a crowd'. 終日 'the whole day'. 及 'to reach'. 好 is hào 'to be fond of' (Zhu Xi: 好去聲). 小慧 'petty cleverness' — Zhu Xi: private cleverness (私智); He Yan's Zheng Xuan: small talents and wits. 難矣哉 'hard — how!'. Zhu Xi: when talk does not reach what is right, the heart of licence, perversity, depravity and extravagance (放辟邪侈) grows; when one is fond of petty cleverness, the springs of walking in danger and trusting to luck (行險僥倖) run hot; 難矣哉 says they have no way into virtue and harm is coming to them. Zheng Xuan: 難矣哉 means they will never achieve anything."),
# ---------------- 15.17 ----------------
dict(pas="15.17", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^君子=jūn zǐ/the gentleman | 義/what is right | 以/(he) takes | 為=wéi/as | 質，/(his) substance | ^禮/(with) the rites | 以/by (them) | 行=xíng/(he) carries out | 之，/it | ^孫=xùn/(with) humility | 以/by (it) | 出/(he) brings forth | 之，/it | ^信/(with) good faith | 以/by (it) | 成/(he) completes | 之。/it | ^君子=jūn zǐ/a gentleman | 哉！」/indeed",
  l="The Master said: “The gentleman takes what is right as his substance,\ncarries it out by the rites,\nbrings it forth with humility,\ncompletes it with good faith.\nA gentleman indeed!”",
  i="The Master said: “The gentleman makes what is right the stuff he is made of; he puts it into practice through the rites, brings it out with modesty, and sees it through with good faith. That is a gentleman!”",
  n="Four clauses of one shape: a noun put first, then 以 'by it' and a verb. 義以為質 is 以義為質 'takes right as substance' with the object brought forward; 禮以行之 is 以禮行之 'by the rites carries it out', and so on. 之 'it' each time is what is right. 質 'plain stuff, substance' (6.16) — Zhu Xi: the trunk (質幹). 行 xíng 'to carry out'. 孫 is xùn, the same word as 遜 'humble, yielding' (Zhu Xi: 孫去聲; He Yan's text writes 遜), as in 14.4. Zhu Xi: what is right is the root of dealing with affairs, so it is the trunk; carrying it out must have measure and pattern; bringing it out must be with modest yielding; completing it must lie in sincerity — that is the gentleman's Way. He quotes Master Cheng: 'right as substance' is like a trunk; the rites carry it out, humility brings it out, good faith completes it; the four lines are one thing only, with right as the root. And again: with reverence straighten the inner, then with right square the outer (敬以直内則義以方外); with right as substance, then the rites to carry it out, humility to bring it out, good faith to complete it. 君子哉 'a gentleman — indeed!' closes with the exclamation of 15.6. He Yan has no comment."),
]
